WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:06.740
Esther Dyson: Like everyone, and perhaps slightly earlier than some, I started noticing

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:15.420
not so much the opioid crisis, which is white people dying, but the problem of addiction.

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:22.240
And just how widespread it was
and it clearly was getting more widespread.

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:33.580
And there seemed to be some relation to that
and the the loss of jobs especially

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:38.480
sort of middle class or slightly lower men.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:42.100
And that you can see in the statistics and so forth.

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:44.940
It felt kind of familiar to me it reminded me of Russia.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:52.740
Where under the Soviets there were people
just doing useless jobs

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:55.360
and drinking themselves to death.

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:59.940
I asked one of my friends, I said, you know
you shouldn't smoke and drink so much

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:06.740
because you're gonna die early. And he said, well, 
life is not so wonderful that we really care about that.

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:10.300
Better to drink now and be happy.

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:15.340
The women tended to deal with it much better.

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:18.240
For two reasons: one, they had children and they felt

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:21.660
they were important in somebody's lives
and had a purpose.

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:26.560
Also they took the money they
and their husbands earned

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:30.400
and went and stood on line and turned the money
into things that they valued.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:35.140
The money itself was useless
and the jobs were purposeless.

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:41.200
So I started reading about addiction.
If anyone is curious, there's Gabor Maté,

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:48.740
there was Maia Szalavitz who wrote
a book called "Unbroken Brain."

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:56.420
Addiction is not a character defect,
she said, it was a learning disorder.

00:01:56.420 --> 00:02:03.020
Then I read the book that I thought made the most sense, called "The Biology of Desire" by Marc Lewis.

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:08.260
He really said, that it was learning FROM disorder.

00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:19.400
In other words, fundamentally, if you start
your life out with a lack of security

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:28.900
with unpredictable parents, with basically... if your brain is damaged during the first few years of life

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:33.180
you become vulnerable later on.

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:40.000
There's lots of stuff now
about adverse childhood experiences

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:45.040
and there's a clear correlation between adverse early childhood experiences and addiction.

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:47.680
obesity, dropping out of school,

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:52.220
abusing your own children, ending up in jail...

00:02:52.220 --> 00:03:00.300
and so forth. So Marc Lewis,
who himself was formerly addicted,

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:03.880
tells the story of five people.

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:08.940
And, towards the end, he says I don't really know what my next book is going to be about.

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:14.280
But one way or another it is about time.

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:17.720
And in a sense foreshortening.

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:24.880
You lose your peripheral vision.
You're focused only on this thing you desire.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:30.100
You're never satiated.
You reach for it, that's the moment of desire.

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:34.180
You get it and you're almost immediately
back in the cycle.

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:41.530
You lose a sense of time. You lose your past that was different. You lose a sense of agency.

00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:44.440
I can have a future that's different.

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:48.580
He talked about Native Americans. The ones who had lost their sense of history

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:53.360
are much more subject to alcohol abuse
and all those familiar problems.

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:58.300
So I went to see him in the Netherlands, where he lives.

00:03:58.300 --> 00:04:05.220
And, this time thing, and this short cycle...

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:07.760
We need dopamine. We need desire to live.

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:12.700
We won't do anything if we don't have a desire...

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:15.840
But if your desire is only short-term

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:20.020
and instant it, it becomes addiction.

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:29.300
I used to think the idea of "cell phone addiction"
was just like a clever metaphor. But, not really.

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:33.820
In fact, addiction is a behavior pattern.

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:43.820
If you're addicted to a substance that has bad physical effects (like alcohol, in particular, or drugs) it's worse.

00:04:43.820 --> 00:04:50.040
But it's the behavior pattern
and that foreshortened cycle of

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:54.580
wanting something that doesn't actually
provide the relief you're seeking

00:04:54.580 --> 00:05:00.020
much more than it is the substance
that's really at the heart of addiction.

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:06.700
The trick is how do you change that
short term desire which is addiction

00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:09.280
into long term desire which is purpose?

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:13.160
Where there is a sense of the future and of change

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:18.460
and of getting some gratification
that's long-term and permanent.

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:22.380
Kara Platoni: So let me ask you, I think there are
some people who would say

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:26.960
Kara: the solution is individual.
A person has to make good choices.

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:31.960
They are responsible agents of their own fortune.

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:34.300
Addiction is a moral failing.

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:37.500
There are others who'd say, no, it's a structural problem.

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:42.720
It's a collective problem. People may not have choices. All of their choices may be bad.

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:45.500
Their environment may be constraining their choices.

00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:48.000
Esther: And they're not making a stupid choice.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:52.380
Esther: If you have no job why not go out and get drunk. You'll feel better.

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:57.000
Kara: Or why not... if the only job in your community
is to deal drugs, then you'll deal drugs.

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:58.690
Kara: if it's the most lucrative thing.

00:05:58.690 --> 00:06:00.940
Esther: And that's different from addiction.

00:06:00.940 --> 00:06:04.760
Esther: The best drug dealers are not addicted.
They're smart business people.

00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:10.700
Kara: But the point is that your environment gives you
a certain set of choices, right?

00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:18.580
Esther: Yes. And you can only make the choices that you have to make.

00:06:18.580 --> 00:06:25.380
Esther: You can get into long discussions,
but fundamentally...

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:29.020
Esther: How many of you are AI people?

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:33.120
We need a better training set for infant brains.

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:40.680
We train our AI's. We need to figure out how
to train our children's brains.

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:46.900
Not with rote training,
but train them that "your mother loves you",

00:06:46.900 --> 00:06:51.140
and that life is not totally secure,

00:06:51.140 --> 00:06:54.080
but your parents love you and they'll take care of you.

00:06:54.080 --> 00:07:00.100
You need to give them a sense of security,
so they can trust in the long term.

00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:06.500
In a sense, addiction is: you've lost trust in everything but this immediate short-term source of solace.

